.SH DESCRIPTION
.I curses
supports color rendering on terminals with applicable capabilities.
Once the library has initialized the terminal,
.B \%has_colors
tells an application whether that terminal type has color capability.
If it does,
calling
.B \%start_color
enables the feature.
(See section \*(``NOTES\*('' below regarding \fB\%ripoffline\fP(3X).)
.PP
When applying colors to a
.I curses
window,
the library manages them in pairs.
A
.I "color pair"
couples a foreground color applied
to the visible strokes of a glyph
with a background color for the field in the character cell
within which the glyph appears.
Configure at least one color pair to use the color feature.
.B \%init_pair
initializes a color pair identifier,
whose value you select,
from a pair of
.I "color indices,"
foreground and background.
Each index represents a color.
X/Open Curses standardizes,
and a
.I curses
library defines,
a small set of color indices;
see section \*(``MACROS\*('' below.
The macro
.B \%COLOR_PAIR(\fIn\fP)
converts a pair
.I n
thus initialized to the value required to encode it in a
.I \%chtype
or
.IR \%attr_t "."
Another macro,
.B \%PAIR_NUMBER(\fIn\fP)
conversely extracts a color pair identifier
from variables of those data types.
.B \%pair_content
permits discovery of a color pair's current definition.
.B \%color_content
extracts the
red,
green,
and blue components of the color using the given index.
.PP
.B \%can_change_color
tells an application whether the terminal type
permits (re)definition of a color.
If it does,
you can call
.B \%init_color
to update the specified color index to use
red,
green,
and blue components of your choice.
.PP
Subsection \*(``Color Handling\*('' of \fB\%terminfo\fP(5)
describes the capabilities that terminal types use to manage color.
.PP
Passing a
.I curses
function
a color index outside the range
.B 0
to
.BR \%COLORS\-1 ","
or a color pair identifier outside the range
.B 0
to
.B \%COLOR_PAIRS\-1
may result in a runtime error.
.B COLORS
corresponds to the terminal type's
.B \%max_colors
.RB \%( colors )
capability,
and
.B \%COLOR_PAIRS
to
.B \%max_pairs
.RB \%( pairs ).
.I \%ncurses
permits specification of a color index of
.B \-1
in certain extended functions to select a
.IR "default color" ";"
see \fB\%use_default_colors\fP(3X).
.PP
Color pair
.B 0
is special;
it denotes \*(``no color\*('',
meaning the terminal's
(typically monochrome)
power-up default fore- and background.
.PP
For each screen,
.I \%ncurses
maintains a
.I "color palette"
that maps color indices into the RGB
(red,
green,
blue)
color space.
